WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.680
Behold.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.140
The foundation of the universe.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.720
The ideas that govern the microscopic world.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:10.000
It’s quantum field theory time.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.360
This episode was supported by SkillShare.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:16.260
Hey Crazies.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.120
I know you’ve wanted quantum field theory
for a while now.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.680
Well, it’s finally time, but let’s ease into
this with a closer look at light.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.420
We’ve got two different models for light:
The EM wave model

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.420
and the photon particle model.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.600
Both of which seem unconnected.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.760
Spoiler Alert: They’re not.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.320
Having ideas be separate is great when you’re
trying to be practical,

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.140
but deeper understanding comes from seeing
how things are the same.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.880
Even though Henry from MinutePhysics has called
me pedantic for this philosophy,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.620
I’m going to stand by it anyway.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.320
Neither of these models is a part of the other.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.420
Like I said, they seem unconnected.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.320
The only possibility is that there’s a deeper
idea that explains both of them.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.060
That deeper idea is the quantum field.

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.120
But let’s ask a simpler question first.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.480
What is a field?

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.420
I did a video on this before, but that was
back in the early days,

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.120
so let’s go over this again.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:12.240
A field is a quantity or measurement assigned
to all points in space.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.000
That’s it. Nothing more. Nothing less.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.100
But quantities come in many different varieties,
kind of like my clones.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.540
There are scalar quantities, which are just
single numbers.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.540
A scalar field would be something like voltage
or electric potential.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.240
A single number assigned to every point in
space.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.660
There are also vector quantities, which are
numbers with direction.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.880
A vector field would be something like an
electric field.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.680
A number and arrow assigned to every point
in space.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.100
There are even tensor quantities, with many
levels of detail.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:47.880
A tensor field would be something like the
Riemann spacetime curvature.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.780
A collection of 256 numbers assigned to each
event in spacetime.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:58.180
Light is called an electromagnetic wave because
it’s a wave in the electromagnetic field.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.800
When I show you a wave like this,
the blue and orange arrows don’t have a

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.300
size in space.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.900
Only the white line does.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:09.250
The arrows are measured in whatever units
are appropriate for the field.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:12.720
A field that existed before the wave passed
through it.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.560
I’ve always just kept the picture simple
by not showing you the field.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.580
Every field has an equilibrium.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.060
In the middle of empty space with nothing
around,

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.600
the equilibrium for the EM field is zero everywhere.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.230
When the light passes through, the field is
disturbed and those numbers change.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:32.520
After the light passes through, the field
returns to normal.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:36.560
The point I’m trying to make is that zero-values
don’t mean the field isn’t there.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.180
Zero is still something.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.700
The field is always there.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:41.680
Well, at least on paper.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:44.240
The physical existence of a field is still
up for debate.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.840
So is that electromagnetic field a quantum
field?

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.400
Eh, not really. At least, not the one I just showed
you.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.000
Oh.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.060
Come to think of it, that electromagnetic
field is actually two fields.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.459
A blue arrow for the electric field and an
orange arrow for the magnetic field.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.850
But when we talk about them, we group them
together as one thing.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:05.600
You might say we unify them.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.400
That’s what a unified theory is:
Showing that two different things are just

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.140
different parts of the same thing.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:14.000
In this case, the electromagnetic field.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.540
This is a tensor that has electric parts and
magnetic parts.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:23.220
Instead of having two different vector fields,
we just have one tensor field.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.540
But tensors aren’t always very convenient.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.400
Hmmm, let’s take this one step deeper.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.980
The electric and magnetic vector fields can
be written as

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:34.140
changes in electric and magnetic potentials.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:39.300
That’s brings our tensor of 16 numbers down
to just a vector with 4.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:43.220
It contains all the same information and,
honestly, it’s much closer to reality.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:44.520
Wait a minute!!

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.900
Potential is almost potential energy, so I
think we’re onto something here.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:54.360
We’ve stepped down from the electromagnetic
field to an energy field.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.900
Energy is something quantum particles can
have.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:00.760
A quantum field is just a field of quantum
properties.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:04.840
A quantum measurement like energy assigned
to every point in space,

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.000
but this is where things get a little wonky.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.140
Quantum properties are intertwined with each
other through the wave function

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:15.160
and, if we imagine that quantum properties
are part of fundamental fields

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.780
then they’re no longer attached to particles.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:21.500
There are 17 different types of particles
with unique properties.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:23.320
Some of those properties are fixed.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:26.940
Others, like spin orientation, have some wiggle-room.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.840
If we start treating their properties like
fields instead

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.520
then we’re going to need, like, 100s of fields.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.920
So many fields, my brain hurts!

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.760
Alright, maybe we can group them together
like we did for the EM field.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:44.980
Let’s focus on some basic quantum numbers:
Spin, Electric Charge, and Color Charge.

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:49.380
Color charge is how we tell gluons apart
from photons, commenters.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:53.580
If we group using those properties, the field-count
drops way down.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.800
Different spins and charges require separate
fields, so

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.760
we’ve got 6 Leptons, 6 Quarks each with
3 possible color-charges,

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.360
8 color-charge combinations for Gluons.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:05.909
Yep, all separate.

00:05:05.909 --> 00:05:10.140
1 Photon, 3 Weak Bosons, and 1 Higgs.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:14.840
If each one gets its own field, that’s a
grand total of 37 fields,

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:18.540
which is the best we can do without a semester-long
course in particle physics.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.199
Wait, then what’s a particle?

00:05:20.199 --> 00:05:21.199
Perfect timing!

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.760
You’re, like, my best clone ever.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:24.020
I’m so proud of you.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:25.040
Really?!

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.140
So these fields exist everywhere, across all
space and time.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.240
But that doesn’t mean particles are everywhere.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.400
There’s only a particle when a quantum field
get excited.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:41.260
For example, this is the photon field with
no photons in sight.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:45.160
Here in empty space, the field-energy hovers
around zero

00:05:45.169 --> 00:05:49.169
with just some quantum uncertainty keeping
it from being exactly zero.

00:05:49.169 --> 00:05:52.720
It’s called the Vacuum State: The ground
state of the field.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.680
Suddenly, an electron and a positron crash
into each other.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:00.100
They annihilate and their energy is added
to the photon field.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:05.320
The result is two photons, two non-zero places
in the photon field.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:09.080
But then, the same can be said about the electron
and positron.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:13.220
They were just places in the electron field
with an energy hovering above zero

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:18.040
and their interaction shoved their energy
into a different field: The photon field.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:21.100
It’s just energy moving from one field to
another.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:25.720
Welcome to Quantum Electrodynamics:
The study of how the electron field and the

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:27.760
photon field interact.

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:30.900
It’s an example of something called a Quantum
Field Theory.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:33.740
A model that governs how quantum fields interact.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:38.180
So to summarize:
A quantum field is a set of quantum properties

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:40.320
assigned to every point in space.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.540
Each one existing alongside the others, allowing
them to interact.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:47.839
and any energy in a field higher than its
vacuum state

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:51.540
will be seen as a particle carrying the properties
of that field.

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:53.060
Whoa!

